13:26:03 | INFO | 🤖 Target: That's a really sharp analogy to reach for, and it shows good engineering intuition—but it also contains a hidden assumption worth checking: most of those entrance "dings" aren't pressure mats either! Many are actually **infrared beam break sensors** or **motion detectors** above the door. The ones that genuinely are pressure-sensitive mats (rubber mats with a switch mechanism inside) do exist, but they're a good case study for *why that approach doesn't scale to roads*. ## Why Pressure/Weight Sensing Works Fine at Store Entrances - Light loads: ~100-250 lbs per person - Low frequency: dozens to hundreds of activations/day - Gentle application: someone walking, not slamming down - Replaceable: if a mat wears out, you swap a $50-100 part in minutes - Indoor: no weather, no temperature swings, no road salt, no snow plows ## Why the Same Approach Struggles at a Road Intersection **Mechanical durability problem:** - Vehicles weigh 300 lbs (motorcycle) to 80,000 lbs (loaded semi)—a much wider range and vastly higher force - Constant heavy impact, repeated thousands of times daily, for years - Any mechanical switch or membrane would wear out or crack under that kind of repeated stress way faster than a solid-state loop **Environmental durability problem:** - Roads face freeze/thaw cycles, snowplow scraping, de-icing salt corrosion, UV degradation, oil/fuel spills - A pressure mat with moving parts or membranes is far more vulnerable to all of this than a sealed wire loop with no moving parts **Installation/maintenance cost:** - Loops are just wire cut into a saw-groove and sealed with epoxy—cheap, and if one fails, you cut a new slot - Complex pressure-sensing infrastructure would need to survive vehicle loads *and* be replaceable without tearing up major intersections for extended repairs ## What They Actually Use When Weight Genuinely Matters Piezoelectric sensors *do* exist in roads—but they're deployed for specialized purposes like **weigh-